tools.description:
Ask a window to close, as Hyprland's close dispatcher does. Close only windows you launched or the user asked you to close. Reports within 2 seconds whether it closed, and lists windows that appeared meanwhile, such as a confirmation dialog, which you can then screenshot and act on.
It refuses X11 menus, lists and tooltips, and X11 windows that take no close request, since closing them through Hyprland ends or breaks their whole app. Dismiss a menu with the key escape or a click outside it.
Example: {"window":"0x55d1c0a3e4b0"}

Start an application on a hidden workspace, without changing the user's visible workspace or focus. Waits up to timeout_ms milliseconds (default 30000) for its first window and for that window to settle, and returns the window's address with a shot; act takes coordinates in that shot's pixels. Close the windows you launch when you are done.
command is the program and its arguments, run without a shell. workspace is a named workspace or the number of a numbered one (default "hyprmasq"). Applications that hand the request to an instance that is already running, such as a browser that is open, cannot be launched this way: pass an option for a separate instance.
Example: {"command":["foot","-T","scratch"]}

Take a screenshot of a window now and return it as a shot such as "s5"; it waits only for a window that has not yet redrawn at a new size, or not yet drawn on its hidden workspace, for up to 1.5 s. Coordinates in later act calls are pixels of the returned image, with 0,0 at its top left; the window's open popups are included.
region zooms in: {shot, x, y, width, height} in pixels of an earlier shot of the same window. The area is captured again at the monitor's full scale, shrunk only to fit within 1280 pixels on the long side, and becomes a new shot whose own pixels act accepts. A zoom under 768 pixels on the long side comes enlarged by a whole factor, each captured pixel a square block, and act maps its pixels to fractions of the captured ones, so you can aim finer. Use it to read small text or to hit small targets.
Example: {"window":"0x55d1c0a3e4b0","region":{"shot":"s4","x":380,"y":60,"width":200,"height":80}}
